{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1214779"},"page_start":"1477","journal_title":"Class.Quant.Grav."},"title":{"title":"Properties of asymptotically flat two-dimensional black holes"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Mann, Robert B."},{"full_name":"Morris, M.S."},{"full_name":"Ross, S.F."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","value":"[42] Robert B. Mann, M. S. Morris, S. F. Ross, \"Properties of asymptotically flat two-dimensional black holes\", Class.Quant.Grav.,10,1477-1494, DOI: 10.1088/0264-9381/10/8/010"}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/319827"}}],"number_of_pages":18,"preprint_date":"2022-02-23","author_count":2,"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"18 pages, 1 figure, comments and references added"}],"earliest_date":"2022-02-23","refereed":true,"external_system_identifiers":[{"schema":"MSNET","value":"4528009"}],"facet_author_name":["1952141_Hwajin Eom","1043125_Wontae Kim"],"core":true,"license":[{"license":"arXiv nonexclusive-distrib 1.0","material":"preprint","url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:2037013","updated":"2024-10-01T15:44:55.352635"},"journal_title_variants":["JCAP"],"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"value":"2202.11327"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["R.B.Mann.1","P.Suranyi.1","T.A.Apostolatos.1","C.Bunster.1","E.Elizalde.1","W.Israel.1","J.M.M.Senovilla.1","Joshua.Eby.1","P.S.Apostolopoulos.1","F.Rahaman.1","E.A.Lim.1","J.P.S.Lemos.1","M.A.H.MacCallum.1","A.A.Garcia.Diaz.1","Tiberio.de.Paula.Netto.1","N.Brouzakis.1","I.Karar.2","Julio.Oliva.1","E.Greenwood.1","A.A.Kehagias.1","Philippe.Spindel.1","M.Rooman.1","J.A.Sierra.Garcia.1","Ranjan.Sharma.1","A.I.Zelnikov.1","An.Zhong.Wang.1","E.Radu.1","J.Zanelli.1","George.Tringas.1","F.C.Mena.1","W.T.Kim.1","A.Di.Prisco.1","A.Vilar.Lopez.1","L.C.R.Wijewardhana.1","J.J.Oh.1","N.Cruz.1","Malcolm.Fairbairn.1","G.Kofinas.1","T.P.Singh.1","Katy.Clough.1","R.Penrose.1","P.Sikivie.1","H.S.Snyder.1","S.D.Odintsov.1","L.Herrera.1","P.S.Joshi.1","S.Hyun.1","M.S.Morris.1","M.Leembruggen.1","J.Robert.Oppenheimer.1","Valeri.P.Frolov.1","S.F.Ross.1","S.Gutti.1","Saibal.Ray.1","Jae.Hoon.Jeong.1","R.Becerril.1","Rong.Gen.Cai.1","E.Tzavara.2","E.Halstead.1","T.Helfer.1","G.T.Horowitz.1","C.Stelea.1","J.D.Edelstein.1","D.L.Welch.2","I.Dalianis.1","N.O.Santos.3","K.A.Bronnikov.1","J.R.Ipser.2","E.Papantonopoulos.1","M.Lubo.1","K.Sfetsos.1","O.B.Zaslavskii.1","M.Banados.1","Kip.S.Thorne.1","Hermann.Bondi.1","X.Hao.5","R.Maulick.2","Raul.Vera.1","N.Tetradis.1","A.Cisterna.1","P.Tod.2","F.Echeverria.1","Paulo.M.Sa.1","D.J.E.Marsh.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"scale.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : ","label":"fig:b","source":"arxiv","key":"c358465d244e7ceacbfe9dc313f89c66","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/c358465d244e7ceacbfe9dc313f89c66"},{"filename":"dilaton.png","material":"preprint","caption":" :  : The decreasing scale factor \\eqref{eq:final_sol_a} and the increasing dilaton field \\eqref{eq:final_sol_phi} on the edge with respect to the comoving time $t$ are plotted by setting $\\ell=r_0=1$, $M=2$, $\\mathcal{R}_0 =3$ and $\\eta=0.7$ for simplicity.","label":"fig:b","source":"arxiv","key":"01dc077cb4e3beed90800f5c3208576a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/01dc077cb4e3beed90800f5c3208576a"}],"inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology","source":"arxiv"},{"term":"Theory-HEP","source":"arxiv"}],"first_author":{"emails":["um16@sogang.ac.kr"],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/05en5nh73"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/056tn4839"}],"full_name":"Eom, Hwajin","last_name":"Eom","first_name":"Hwajin","recid":1952141},"control_number":2037013,"dois":[{"source":"IOP","value":"10.1088/1475-7516/2022/11/004"},{"material":"publication","source":"arXiv","value":"10.1088/1475-7516/2022/11/004"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Eom:2022duc"],"abstracts":[{"source":"IOP","value":"In three-dimensional low-energy string theory,\nwe study the formation of a black string from a dust cloud.\nWe analytically obtain two distinct classes of exact solutions with arbitrary functions\nresponsible for mass distributions of\nthe dust cloud. The first and second kinds of solutions may describe collapsing dusts but\nthe first kind is only for inhomogeneous dust distribution while the second kind has a homogeneous limit.\nThe finite collapse time and the Israel junction conditions tell us that\nthe first kind solution describes\na desired collapsing phenomenon, whereas\nthe scale factor in the inner spacetime for the second kind\nturns out to be trivial.\nIn the first kind solution, specific collapsing models can be realized by choosing an\nappropriate inhomogeneous\ndust distribution consistent with the Israel junction conditions.\nConsequently, the inhomogeneous dust cloud eventually collapses to the\nblack string although the homogeneous dust cloud\ndoes not guarantee the formation of the black string in our setting.\nThe space-like curvature singularities occur at the finite collapse time and\nthey can be cloaked by the horizon of the black string.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"IOP"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"In three-dimensional low-energy string theory, we study the formation of a black string from a dust cloud. We analytically obtain two distinct classes of exact solutions with arbitrary functions responsible for mass distributions of the dust cloud. The first and second kinds of solutions may describe collapsing dusts but the first kind is only for inhomogeneous dust distribution while the second kind has a homogeneous limit. The finite collapse time and the Israel junction conditions tell us that the first kind solution describes a desired collapsing phenomenon, whereas the scale factor in the inner spacetime for the second kind turns out to be trivial. In the first kind solution, specific collapsing models can be realized by choosing an appropriate inhomogeneous dust distribution consistent with the Israel junction conditions. Consequently, the inhomogeneous dust cloud eventually collapses to the black string although the homogeneous dust cloud does not guarantee the formation of the black string in our setting. The space-like curvature singularities occur at the finite collapse time and they can be cloaked by the horizon of the black string.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["gr-qc"],"titles":[{"source":"IOP","title":"Analytic approach to the formation of a three-dimensional black string from a dust cloud"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Analytic approach to the formation of a three-dimensional black string from a dust cloud"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2022-11-04"}],"curated":true}},{"created":"2021-07-05T03:04:18.772526+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1875723?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1875723?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1875723?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1875723?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1875723?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1875723?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A1875723"},"updated":"2023-03-07T00:25:39.921513+00:00","id":"1875723","metadata":{"authors":[{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Department of Atmospheric Sciences, Texas A&M University, College Station, TX 77843, USA"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"ding, jiachen","full_name":"Ding, Jiachen","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1875724"},"ids":[{"schema":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4928-6698"},{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"J.Ding.1"}],"last_name":"Ding","signature_block":"DANGj","first_name":"Jiachen","uuid":"bb0ceb8c-9419-4772-b731-b83ce61a40a5","recid":1875724},{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Department of Physics and Astronomy, Texas A&M U