ert.T.Fisher.1","Gianfranco.Brunetti.1","M.Lopez.Corredoira.1","F.Nakamura.1","P.Sharma.3","M.D.Gladders.1","C.Federrath.1","S.Gupta.13","G.Bodo.2","R.Scranton.1","R.S.Klessen.5","S.Massaglia.5","F.Vazza.1","M.Roncarelli.2","M.Vogelsberger.1","M.Sharma.1","M.L.Balogh.5","M.A.de.Avillez.2","Debora.Sijacki.1","L.Montier.1","B.D.Savage.1","G.T.Richards.1","D.Chelouche.1","S.Y.Wu.7","C.Klingenberg.1","P.Colella.2","Y.A.Shchekinov.2","Brice.Menard.1","S.Bianchi.4","Philip.F.Hopkins.1","S.McGee.1","L.K.Konstandin.1","E.Pointecouteau.1","A.S.R.Gonzalez.1","B.B.Nath.1","E.O.Vasiliev.2","B.C.Hsieh.2","J.N.Bregman.1","Roser.Pello.1","A.Mignone.2","S.Muller.3","D.Nelson.2","E.HodgesKluck.6","A.N.Youdin.1","Carlos.M.Gutierrez.1","B.P.Koester.1","B.J.Vaidya.1","R.I.Klein.1","J.C.Howk.1","C.F.McKee.1","A.Johansen.3","V.Springel.1","M.Fukugita.1","Hyunseok.Lee.1","W.Schmidt.6","S.C.O.Glover.4","Shy.Genel.1","P.Rossi.6","W.Schmidt.5","Laurent.Loinard.1","A.Ferrara.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"mcolor100-evol-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"{\\it {Left}}. {Mass} fraction of colored gas during the crushing of a cloud by a shockwave for the tracer concentration levels $C > 0.01$, 0.1, 0.3, 0.9 (color lines). {\\it {Right}}. Mass fraction of colored gas for the runs with different spatial resolution (the ratio of the cell size to the cloud radius {is shown in the legend}) for $C > 0.1$. The mass of gas tracked by Lagrangian particles is shown by black line: it remains constant. The masses are normalized to the initial mass of a cloud $M_0$. The time is in units of crushing timescale $t_{cc}=3$ Myr for the parameters adopted here.","label":"fig-mcolor","source":"arxiv","key":"6e033d4d0c94b2e1358d758214b63d34","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/6e033d4d0c94b2e1358d758214b63d34"},{"filename":"mcolor100-evol-vs-resol-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"{\\it {Left}}. {Mass} fraction of colored gas during the crushing of a cloud by a shockwave for the tracer concentration levels $C > 0.01$, 0.1, 0.3, 0.9 (color lines). {\\it {Right}}. Mass fraction of colored gas for the runs with different spatial resolution (the ratio of the cell size to the cloud radius {is shown in the legend}) for $C > 0.1$. The mass of gas tracked by Lagrangian particles is shown by black line: it remains constant. The masses are normalized to the initial mass of a cloud $M_0$. The time is in units of crushing timescale $t_{cc}=3$ Myr for the parameters adopted here.","label":"fig-mcolor","source":"arxiv","key":"d543247e346db3c15183db111bec6d69","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/d543247e346db3c15183db111bec6d69"},{"filename":"sn-2d-slice-1.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Density and temperature of gas, color, density of Lagrangian particles, density of dust particles in logarithmic scale ({from left to right panels}) at $t=2.4$ Myr $=0.8t_{cc}$. The upper panels present the slices for the fiducial model (the highest resolution) with $d_{cell}/r_{c,0} = 1/30$, and the lower panels show the maps for the model with the lowest resolution $d_{cell}/r_{c,0} = 1/10$.","label":"fig-maps","source":"arxiv","key":"f207c42e400187e3d249cdcce94a662f","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f207c42e400187e3d249cdcce94a662f"},{"filename":"sn-2d-slice-1l.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Density and temperature of gas, color, density of Lagrangian particles, density of dust particles in logarithmic scale ({from left to right panels}) at $t=2.4$ Myr $=0.8t_{cc}$. The upper panels present the slices for the fiducial model (the highest resolution) with $d_{cell}/r_{c,0} = 1/30$, and the lower panels show the maps for the model with the lowest resolution $d_{cell}/r_{c,0} = 1/10$.","label":"fig-maps","source":"arxiv","key":"ef48d9dfb8fd7a13bee1266ff0ae0fe5","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/ef48d9dfb8fd7a13bee1266ff0ae0fe5"},{"filename":"mgas100-evol-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"\\it {Left}}. Mass fractions of a gas in the cloud tracked by colored fluid for $C > 0.1$ (thin {solid} lines), {$C > 0.3$ (thin dashed lines)}, and Lagrangian particles (thick lines) for different thermal phases (the labels correspond to the logarithm of gas temperature). The mass of gas is normalized to the total mass of the cloud at the current time: $M_t(t)$. {\\it {Right}}. {The averaged (mass-weighted) velocity for the mass fractions of a gas depicted in left panel.","label":"fig-lps","source":"arxiv","key":"a1ad53890253966aed2ae1ea6d14887d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/a1ad53890253966aed2ae1ea6d14887d"},{"filename":"vgas100-evol-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"\\it {Left}}. Mass fractions of a gas in the cloud tracked by colored fluid for $C > 0.1$ (thin {solid} lines), {$C > 0.3$ (thin dashed lines)}, and Lagrangian particles (thick lines) for different thermal phases (the labels correspond to the logarithm of gas temperature). The mass of gas is normalized to the total mass of the cloud at the current time: $M_t(t)$. {\\it {Right}}. {The averaged (mass-weighted) velocity for the mass fractions of a gas depicted in left panel.","label":"fig-lps","source":"arxiv","key":"4ed700d640172fb3046c4ecba29685af","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/4ed700d640172fb3046c4ecba29685af"},{"filename":"mdust100-evol-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"{\\it {Left}}. Mass fractions of dust contained within a gas of the different thermal phases, $M_{d}(T)/M_{d,tot}(t)$ ($M_{d,tot}(t)$ the total mass of dust in a cloud at the current time). {The notations and line styles are the same as in Figure \\ref{fig-lps}.} The dust mass fraction $M_{d,tot}(t)/M_{d,tot}(t=0)$ depicted by dashed line is shown in right axis. {\\it {Right}}. The averaged (mass-weighted) velocity for the mass fractions of dust depicted in left panel.","label":"fig-dps","source":"arxiv","key":"56c35cb25213cd3e92cdc8d12e3f6018","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/56c35cb25213cd3e92cdc8d12e3f6018"},{"filename":"vdust100-evol-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"{\\it {Left}}. Mass fractions of dust contained within a gas of the different thermal phases, $M_{d}(T)/M_{d,tot}(t)$ ($M_{d,tot}(t)$ the total mass of dust in a cloud at the current time). {The notations and line styles are the same as in Figure \\ref{fig-lps}.} The dust mass fraction $M_{d,tot}(t)/M_{d,tot}(t=0)$ depicted by dashed line is shown in right axis. {\\it {Right}}. The averaged (mass-weighted) velocity for the mass fractions of dust depicted in left panel.","label":"fig-dps","source":"arxiv","key":"a7575daa7e0dfea4c1281eaa13668a41","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/a7575daa7e0dfea4c1281eaa13668a41"},{"filename":"pdf-T-c100-26-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Mass fraction distribution functions of temperature (left), number density (middle), velocity (right) of a cloud material tracked by colored fluid (red lines, {labeled 'clr'}), Lagrangian particles (green lines, {labeled 'lps'}), and dust particles (blue lines, {labeled 'dps'}) at time moments {$5t_{cc}$ (top row), $10t_{cc}$ (bottom row)}.","label":"fig-pdf100","source":"arxiv","key":"6d37656706b02436df10b87fef7741dc","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/6d37656706b02436df10b87fef7741dc"},{"filename":"pdf-n-c100-26-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Mass fraction distribution functions of temperature (left), number density (middle), velocity (right) of a cloud material tracked by colored fluid (red lines, {labeled 'clr'}), Lagrangian particles (green lines, {labeled 'lps'}), and dust particles (blue lines, {labeled 'dps'}) at time moments {$5t_{cc}$ (top row), $10t_{cc}$ (bottom row)}.","label":"fig-pdf100","source":"arxiv","key":"39954b6ed9682c7985ba0551adecf986","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/39954b6ed9682c7985ba0551adecf986"},{"filename":"pdf-v-c100-26-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Mass fraction distribution functions of temperature (left), number density (middle), velocity (right) of a cloud material tracked by colored fluid (red lines, {labeled 'clr'}), Lagrangian particles (green lines, {labeled 'lps'}), and dust particles (blue lines, {labeled 'dps'}) at time moments {$5t_{cc}$ (top row), $10t_{cc}$ (bottom row)}.","label":"fig-pdf100","source":"arxiv","key":"fcbf1bd7865ebcc36a6d83058b038351","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/fcbf1bd7865ebcc36a6d83058b038351"},{"filename":"pdf-T-c100-51-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Mass fraction distribution functions of temperature (left), number density (middle), velocity (right) of a cloud material tracked by colored fluid (red lines, {labeled 'clr'}), Lagrangian particles (green lines, {labeled 'lps'}), and dust particles (blue lines, {labeled 'dps'}) at time moments {$5t_{cc}$ (top row), $10t_{cc}$ (bottom row)}.","label":"fig-pdf100","source":"arxiv","key":"cd90290b3d5028d7e264cc6900a08a7c","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/cd90290b3d5028d7e264cc6900a08a7c"},{"filename":"pdf-n-c100-51-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Mass fraction distribution functions of temperature (left), number density (middle), velocity (right) of a cloud material tracked by colored fluid (red lines, {labeled 'clr'}), Lagrangian particles (green lines, {labeled 'lps'}), and dust particles (blue lines, {labeled 'dps'}) at time moments {$5t_{cc}$ (top row), $10t_{cc}$ (bottom row)}.","label":"fig-pdf100","source":"arxiv","key":"af46d2ad07907fa60d63c3a7c2e409ba","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/af46d2ad07907fa60d63c3a7c2e409ba"},{"filename":"pdf-v-c100-51-paper2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Mass fraction distribution functions of temperature (left), number density (middle), velocity (right) of a cloud material tracked by colored fluid (red lines, {labeled 'clr'}), Lagrangian particles (green lines, {labeled 'lps'}), and dust particles (blue lines, {labeled 'dps'}) at time moments {$5t_{cc}$ (top row), $10t_{cc}$ (bottom row)}.","label":"fig-pdf100","source":"arxiv","key":"b6e4bdb932450aca7aaa9ab347eea2ed","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b6e4bdb932450aca7aaa9ab347eea2ed"},{"filename":"sn-2d-slice-a1.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Density and temperature of gas, color, density of Lagrangian particles, density of dust particles in logarithmic scale (from {left to right panels}) at $t=1t_{cc}$ ({top panels}) $t=10t_{cc}$ ({lower~panels}).","label":"fig-maps-a","source":"arxiv","key":"49f60ee14c76a9954df192d0528c9c65","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/49f60ee14c76a9954df192d0528c9c65"},{"filename":"sn-2d-slice-a.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Density and temperature of gas, color, density of Lagrangian particles, density of dust particles in logarithmic scale (from {left to right panels}) at $t=1t_{cc}$ ({top panels}) $t=10t_{cc}$ ({lower~panels}).","label":"fig-maps-a","source":"arxiv","key":"0a5ca66d9a1c885f9630a3ef539cb30a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/0a5ca66d9a1c885f9630a3ef539cb30a"},{"filename":"mcolor100-a-evol-paper.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Mass fraction of colored gas during the crushing of an adiabatic cloud by a shockwave for the tracer concentration levels $C > 0.01$, 0.1, 0.3, 0.9 (color lines). The mass of gas tracked by Lagrangian particles is shown by black line: it remains constant. The masses are normalized to the initial mass of a cloud $M_0$. The time is in units of crushing timescale $t_{cc}=100$ kyr for the parameters adopted here.","label":"fig-mcolor-a","source":"arxiv","key":"1bf1302c882003387644435c9cb40e4a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/1bf1302c882003387644435c9cb40e4a"}],"inspire_categories":[{"term":"Astrophysics","source":"arxiv"}],"first_author":{"full_name":"Dedikov, Svyatoslav","last_name":"Dedikov","ids":[{"schema":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9971-9776"},{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"S.Y.Dedikov.1"}],"first_name":"Svyatoslav","recid":2571406},"control_number":2772949,"dois":[{"source":"MDPI","value":"10.3390/universe10040155"},{"material":"publication","source":"arXiv","value":"10.3390/universe10040155"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Dedikov:2024zcc"],"abstracts":[{"source":"MDPI","value":"The destructionof clouds by strong shocks and hot winds is the key process responsible for the transporting of metals and dust from the ISM to the ICM/IGM, and establishing the multiphase structure in and around galaxies. In this work, we perform a detailed analysis of this process using two different approaches for tracking the cloud material (gas and dust): the so-called ‘colored’ fluid, and the Lagrangian (trace) particles. We find that for the clouds in the hot phase ( K), the two methods produce significantly different mass fractions and velocities of the cloud material. In contrast, the two methods produce similar results for the clouds that are in the warm/cold phases ( K). We find that the Kelvin–Helmholtz instability is suppressed in the warm clouds of size ∼100 pc and metallicity Z 0.1Z$_{⊙}$ due to effective gas cooling. This causes a delay in the destruction of such clouds that are interacting with the hot ICM flow. We demonstrate that the dust particles that are evacuated from their ‘parent’ clouds to the hot medium show different dynamics when compared to that of the Lagrangian (trace) particles. Our results indicate that the dust grains swept out to the hot gas are destroyed.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"MDPI"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"The destruction of clouds by strong shocks and hot winds is the key process responsible for the transp orting of metals and dust from the ISM to the ICM/IGM, and establishing the multiphase structure in and around galaxies. In this work, we perform a detailed analysis of this process using two different approaches for tracking the cloud material (gas and dust): the so-called 'colored' fluid, and the Lagrangian (trace) particles. We find that for the clouds in the hot phase ($T>10^5$ K), the two methods produce significantly different mass fractions and velocities of the cloud material. In contrast, the two methods produce similar results for the clouds that are in the warm/cold phases ($T < 10^5$ K). We find that the Kelvin--Helmholtz instability is suppressed in the warm clouds of size $\\sim$100 pc and metallicity $Z\\gt 0.1 Z_\\odot$ due to effective gas cooling. This causes a delay in the destruction of such clouds that are interacting with the hot ICM flow. We demonstrate that the dust particles that are evacuated from their 'parent' clouds to the hot medium show different dynamics when compared to that of the Lagrangian (trace) particles. Our results indicate that the dust grains swept out to the hot gas are destroyed.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["astro-ph.GA"],"titles":[{"source":"MDPI","title":"Tracking Dusty Cloud Crushed by a Hot Flow"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Tracking Dusty Cloud Crushed by a Hot Flow"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2024-03-26"}],"curated":false}},{"created":"2010-10-17T00:00:00+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/872960?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/872960?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/872960?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/872960?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/872960?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/872960?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A872960"},"updated":"2023-03-07T01:00:11.095530+00:00","id":"872960","metadata":{"authors":[{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"paradis, d.","full_name":"Paradis, D.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/2189235"},"last_name":"Paradis","ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"D.Paradis.1"}],"signature_block":"PARADd","uuid":"43f40a75-33d9-4e57-a459-3df0bf87248c","first_name":"D.","recid":2189235},{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"reach, w.t.","full_name":"Reach, W.T.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1031003"},"last_nam