Eichhorn.1","I.S.Glass.4","W.B.Burton.4","J.Rho.2","R.Jungo.1","A.Kawamura.1","S.H.Moseley.1","David.B.Sanders.1","S.i.Asayama.1","C.R.McCreight.1","S.Nishiyama.4","Daniel.Stern.1","A.Hara.1","T.R.Bontekoe.1","W.J.Forrest.2","S.Mano.1","M.D.Fillipovic.1","P.R.Wood.2","S.Wachter.5","B.T.Soifer.9","Iain.McDonald.1","Kenneth.C.Freeman.1","P.Losch.1","A.E.Piatti.5","G.H.Rieke.1","P.J.Lowrance.1","C.Nagashima.6","A.AlonsoHerrero.12","F.van.Leeuwen.6","A.NoriegaCrespo.3","E.Mueller.9","J.L.Hinz.6","R.Kichak.1","C.D.Laney.1","G.G.Fazio.1","E.V.Tollestrup.1","K.Volk.2","S.S.Kim.4","M.J.Kesteven.2","Casey.Papovich.1","J.Cadien.2","S.J.Carey.2","S.S.Kim.2","Paul.S.Smith.1","T.Kawai.5","N.Kaneyasu.1","M.E.McKelvey.1","T.Zenno.1","E.E.Haller.2"],"figures":[{"filename":"fig4_341808fig1.png","caption":"Temperature histograms in the atomic (black) and molecular phase (grey).","source":"arxiv","key":"99a88acf45f4f81d9cc2fd38cdffc5e7","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/99a88acf45f4f81d9cc2fd38cdffc5e7"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig2.png","caption":"Map of the intensity of the ISRF in the atomic phase, overlaid with the NANTEN $^{12}$CO (J=1-0) integrated intensity contours convolved to the 4$^{\\prime}$ resolution, at 0.5, 2, 4 and 8 K km s$^{-1}$.","source":"arxiv","key":"f8f2df5469a840d19b15bcce6c9a7d44","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f8f2df5469a840d19b15bcce6c9a7d44"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig3.png","caption":"Map of the intensity of the ISRF in the molecular phase, overlaid with the NANTEN $^{12}$CO (J=1-0) integrated intensity contours convolved to the 4$^{\\prime}$ resolution, at 0.5, 2, 4 and 8 K km s$^{-1}$.","source":"arxiv","key":"7e1601ca8283b8e463e5d4f13085a489","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/7e1601ca8283b8e463e5d4f13085a489"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig4.png","caption":"Histograms of the dust abundances in the atomic phase in black and molecular phase in grey. From top to bottom: BG, VSG and PAH abundances. The dashed vertical lines are the abundances derived in \\citet{Bernard08} for the solar neighborhood.","source":"arxiv","key":"d4736f5e039ba0d2de0a39707ec3ebc1","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/d4736f5e039ba0d2de0a39707ec3ebc1"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig5.png","caption":"Histograms of the logarithm of the $\\rm \\frac{Y_{PAH}}{Y_{BG}}$ (grey) and $\\rm \\frac{Y_{VSG}}{Y_{BG}}$ (black) ratios between the molecular and the atomic phase.","source":"arxiv","key":"0ff0affcb71431eee64188c5599b6d9c","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/0ff0affcb71431eee64188c5599b6d9c"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig6.png","caption":"Histograms of the logarithm of the $\\rm \\frac{Y_{PAH}}{Y_{BG}}$ (grey) and $\\rm \\frac{Y_{VSG}}{Y_{BG}}$ (black) ratios between the molecular and the atomic phase, for clouds which dust temperature in the molecular phase is colder than in the atomic phase.","source":"arxiv","key":"5891fe0c787d368c8dc3588d11183a51","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5891fe0c787d368c8dc3588d11183a51"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig7.png","caption":"Histograms of the logarithm of the $\\rm \\frac{Y_{PAH}}{Y_{BG}}$ (grey) and $\\rm \\frac{Y_{VSG}}{Y_{BG}}$ (black) ratios between the molecular and the atomic phase, for clouds which dust temperature in the molecular phase is warmer than in the atomic phase.","source":"arxiv","key":"247f2506555d584f29bf02a9a034dc86","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/247f2506555d584f29bf02a9a034dc86"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig8.png","caption":"Histograms of the logarithm of: (A) the dust emissivity at 160 $\\mic$ in cm$^2$/H, in the atomic phase (black) and molecular phase (grey), (B) the extinction over column density ratio in mag H$^{-1}$ cm$^2$ in the atomic phase (black) and molecular phase (grey), (C) the incident luminosity coming from the ISRF, (D) the total luminosity emitted by dust, (E) the ratio of the total luminosity emitted by dust to the luminosity coming from the ISRF, (F) the cloud mass, and (H) the ratio of the total luminosity emitted by dust to the cloud mass. The luminosities and masses are given in unit of solar luminosity and solar mass, respectively.  The dashed vertical lines are the reference values in the solar neighborhood, quoted in Section \\ref{sec_dep}.","source":"arxiv","key":"30fbc24bd0c34903c0dd9f25100a8769","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/30fbc24bd0c34903c0dd9f25100a8769"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig9.png","caption":"Map of the ratio between the total luminosity emitted by dust and the ISRF luminosity ($\\rm L^{Dust}_{TOT}/L_{ISRF}$), overlaid with the NANTEN $^{12}$CO (J=1-0) integrated intensity contours convolved to the 4$^{\\prime}$ resolution, at 0.5, 2, 4 and 8 K km s$^{-1}$. The map is in logarithmic scale. Clouds with no values are those for which the $\\rm L^{Dust}_{TOT}/L_{ISRF}$ ratio could not be derived.","source":"arxiv","key":"50104e8f546ecfe9c01fde7e70dd60a5","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/50104e8f546ecfe9c01fde7e70dd60a5"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig10.png","caption":"Plot of the ratio between the total luminosity emitted by dust and the cloud mass as a function of the cloud mass.","source":"arxiv","key":"f7fd9ff18415004da6a3646d35a114fe","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f7fd9ff18415004da6a3646d35a114fe"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig11.png","caption":"Plot of the ratio between the total luminosity emitted by dust and the cloud mass as a function of the dust temperature in the molecular phase.","source":"arxiv","key":"85a7fb5df3c53b3b11793afc5ec8276c","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/85a7fb5df3c53b3b11793afc5ec8276c"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig12.png","caption":"Map of the ratio between the total luminosity emitted by dust to the cloud mass ($\\rm L^{Dust}_{TOT}/M_{gas}$). The map is in logarithmic scale. The overlaid contours are described in the caption of Figure \\ref{fig_lum_rap}. Clouds with no values are those for which the $\\rm L^{Dust}_{TOT}/M_{gas}$ ratio could not be derived. Boxes I to IV correspond to the stages of the molecular clouds identified following the description given by \\citet{Yamaguchi01}.","source":"arxiv","key":"f1bb0b55c9504d441eab930024bc7789","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f1bb0b55c9504d441eab930024bc7789"},{"filename":"fig4_341808fig13.png","caption":"Ratio of the total luminosity emitted by dust to the cloud mass as a function of the stage of the cloud evolution, for each selected cloud (diamonds), following the description given by \\citet{Yamaguchi01}. The mean value of the ratio for each stage is shown by asterisks linked by the continuous line.","source":"arxiv","key":"66044d8b8c15db41ccbcd2f9f5b83bd7","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/66044d8b8c15db41ccbcd2f9f5b83bd7"}],"legacy_version":"20200413095650.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Astrophysics"}],"first_author":{"full_name":"Paradis, D.","last_name":"Paradis","first_name":"D.","recid":2189235},"control_number":872960,"dois":[{"source":"ADS","value":"10.1088/0004-6256/141/2/43"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Paradis:2010vm"],"abstracts":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"The objective of this paper is to construct a catalog providing the dust properties and the star formation efficiency (SFE) of the molecular clouds in the Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC). We use the infrared (IR) data obtained with the Spitzer telescope as part of the ``Surveying the Agents of a Galaxy's Evolution'' (SAGE) Legacy survey as well as the IRAS data. We also work with extinction (Av) maps of the LMC. A total of 272 molecular clouds have been detected in the LMC in a previous molecular survey, accounting for 230 giant molecular clouds and 42 smaller clouds. We perform correlations between the IR emission/extinction, and atomic and molecular gas tracers. We compare the atomic gas that surrounds the molecular cloud with the molecular gas in the cloud. Using a dust emission model, we derive the physical properties of dust in and outside the clouds: equilibrium temperature, emissivity and extinction. We also determine the luminosity of the interstellar radiation field intercepted by the cloud, and the total IR luminosity. Statistically, we do not find any significant difference in the dust properties between the atomic and the molecular phases. In particular we do not find evidence for a systematic decrease of the dust temperature in the molecular phase, with respect to the surrounding, presumably atomic gas. This is probably because giant molecular clouds are the sites of star formation, which heat the dust, while the smallest clouds are unresolved. The ratio between the IR luminosity and the cloud mass (LDust/Mgas) does not seem to correlate with Mgas. The highest value of the ratio we derived is 18.1 Lsol/Msol in the 30 Doradus region, which is known to be the most prominent star formation region of the LMC, while the most likely value is 0.5 and is representative of quiescent clouds. We provide a prescription to associate the various stages of star formation with its LDust/Mgas.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["astro-ph.GA"],"titles":[{"title":"Statistical study of dust properties in LMC molecular clouds"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Statistical study of dust properties in LMC molecular clouds"}],"curated":false}},{"created":"2005-11-28T00:00:00+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/698867?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/698867?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/698867?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/698867?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/698867?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/698867?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A698867"},"updated":"2023-03-06T19:22:55.870944+00:00","id":"698867","metadata":{"authors":[{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"ridderstad, marianna","full_name":"Ridderstad, Marianna","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/2579871"},"