.net/files/1362009a5069de1f983d5483d8e27c23"},{"filename":"f3a_3d.png","caption":"Infrared maps at 8, 24, 70 and 160$\\mu$m in the Leo Cloud region with the sources removed and convolved to the H{\\sc i} resolution.  The H{\\sc i} contours at 0.1, 0.5, 1.1, 2.2 and $3.8\\times 10^{20}$ at cm$^{-2}$ are overlaid.","source":"arxiv","key":"29c695c24328e8bb7c186503fe08e9fd","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/29c695c24328e8bb7c186503fe08e9fd"},{"filename":"f4a.png","caption":"Average dust emission (and the associated error) in the background region and in 5 H{\\sc i} bins in the region of the Leo Intergalactic Cloud","source":"arxiv","key":"98fa5ba0dbb2066a4fdd253cdc6df29d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/98fa5ba0dbb2066a4fdd253cdc6df29d"},{"filename":"f4b.png","caption":"Average dust emission (and the associated error) in the background region and in 5 H{\\sc i} bins in the region of the Leo Intergalactic Cloud","source":"arxiv","key":"a03824f479b4e4656898bce5f6f8111f","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/a03824f479b4e4656898bce5f6f8111f"},{"filename":"f5.png","caption":"Close-up on the 8$\\mu$m IRAC image where emission associated with the H{\\sc i} emission is detected. The H{\\sc i} contours are overlaid with levels as in Fig. \\ref{fig3}. The infrared emission shows a similar spatial distribution to the highest H{\\sc i} contours, but the peaks in emission are slightly offset.","source":"arxiv","key":"ae90046fcaf07890006049d0189c46e8","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/ae90046fcaf07890006049d0189c46e8"},{"filename":"f6.png","caption":"Result from the cross correlation analysis between the 8$\\mu$m and the H{\\sc i} maps in the field of the highest density structure in the Leo cloud. The central position is noted by a cross and corresponds to where the maximum of correlation would be expected if the spatial distributions were similar.","source":"arxiv","key":"579429d80a106b2dc7c11a1f01040678","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/579429d80a106b2dc7c11a1f01040678"},{"filename":"f7.png","caption":"Spectral energy distribution of dust emission associated with the densest H{\\sc i} in the Leo intergalactic cloud (black triangles and upper limits). It is compared to the spectral energy distribution observed in the solar neighborhood (curves and points in grey), scaled to a H{\\sc i} column density of $4\\times 10^{20} at.cm^{-2}$ and divided by a factor of 15.","source":"arxiv","key":"7cd0e5eb4a2551ce278cebdf99d67119","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/7cd0e5eb4a2551ce278cebdf99d67119"},{"filename":"f8.png","caption":"Spectral energy distributions of the radiation fields heating the clump where dust is detected (left panel) and resulting estimated dust emission (right panel), scaled by a factor of 5 and to an H{\\sc i} column density of $4\\times 10^{20} at.cm^{-2}$ to fit the observed infrared emission (diamonds).","source":"arxiv","key":"5bf37f77bdeeb7029c58b38d8620c2a5","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5bf37f77bdeeb7029c58b38d8620c2a5"}],"legacy_version":"20170220222915.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Astrophysics"}],"first_author":{"full_name":"Bot, C.","last_name":"Bot","first_name":"C.","recid":2026792},"control_number":820722,"dois":[{"source":"ADS","value":"10.1088/0004-6256/138/2/452"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Bot:2009cj"],"abstracts":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"We present a search for infrared dust emission associated with the Leo cloud, a large intergalactic cloud in the M96 group. Mid-infrared and far-infrared images were obtained with IRAC and MIPS on the Spitzer Space Telescope. Our analysis of these maps is done at each wavelength relative to the HI spatial distribution. We observe a probable detection at 8 microns and a marginal detection at 24 microns associated with the highest HI column densities in the cloud. At 70 and 160 microns, upper limits on the dust emission are deduced. The level of the detection is low so that the possibility of a fortuitous cirrus clump or of an overdensity of extragalactic sources along the line of sight can not be excluded. If this detection is confirmed, the quantities of dust inferred imply a dust to gas ratio in the intergalactic cloud up to a few times solar but no less than 1/20 solar. A confirmed detection would therefore exclude the possibility that the intergalactic cloud has a primordial origin. Instead, this large intergalactic cloud could therefore have been formed through interactions between galaxies in the group.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["astro-ph.CO"],"titles":[{"title":"A Search for Dust Emission in the Leo Intergalactic Cloud"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"A Search for Dust Emission in the Leo Intergalactic Cloud"}],"curated":false}},{"created":"2012-03-06T00:00:00+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1093023?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1093023?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1093023?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1093023?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1093023?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1093023?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A1093023"},"updated":"2023-03-06T19:47:12.594200+00:00","id":"1093023","metadata":{"authors":[{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"jeffers, s.v.","full_name":"Jeffers, S.V.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1030637"},"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"S.V.Jeffers.1"}],"last_name":"Jeffers","signature_block":"JAFARs","uuid":"4eb2262f-5c28-48fd-a3c0-51db6b96be02","first_name":"S.V.","recid":1030637},{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"min, m.","full_name":"Min, M.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/2311821"},"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"M.Min.7"}],"last_name":"Min","signature_block":"MANm","uuid":"81c020fa-0be5-4b58-be29-c116c0b225f5","fir