.Donzelli.1","J.Aumont.1","Y.Damerdji.5","N.Krachmalnicoff.2","Laurence.Perotto.1","S.Masi.1","S.R.Hildebrandt.1","A.Melchiorri.1","H.K.Eriksen.1","J.Knude.2","R.Andrae.2","A.J.Banday.1","A.N.Lasenby.1","Kate.D.Alexander.1","M.Kontizas.4","M.Tomasi.1","Theodore.S.Kisner.1","M.P.Hobson.2","P.Vielva.1","D.Alina.1","A.M.Magalhaes.2","C.L.Wong.1","G.Joncas.1","H.U.Norgaard.Nielsen.1","A.Gruppuso.1","E.Pointecouteau.1","C.Dickinson.1","Howard.Hui.1","I.Ristorcelli.1","E.Battaner.1","A.Turner.1","J.Delabrouille.1","Francois.R.Bouchet.1","A.Hornstrup.1","D.C.Sutton.1","F.Thevenin.6","Fabio.Finelli.1","S.Mitra.1","A.De.Rosa.2","C.C.Kuo.2","D.Herranz.1","A.Dapergolas.6","M.Savelainen.1","Joanna.Dunkley.1","J.M.Delouis.1","A.Lobel.9"],"figures":[{"filename":"PitfallsCartoon1.png","caption":"Two clouds with temperatures $T_1$ and $T_2$, dust column densities $\\Sigma_1$ and $\\Sigma_2$, and magnetic fields oriented at different directions contribute to the total dust-emission intensity along a line of sight.","source":"arxiv","key":"e0f454a9176ab535985017a5be446cf0","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e0f454a9176ab535985017a5be446cf0"},{"filename":"r350o3150.png","caption":"Upper panel: ratio of $r$ values at 350 GHz and 150 GHz as a function of cloud 2 temperature, for three different cloud 1 temperatures. Lower panel: Ratio $r=I_1/I_2$ at 150 GHz (black) and 350 GHz (red), as a function of cloud 2 temperature. Cloud 1 temperature is fixed at 20 K. Line type corresponds to different values of $\\Sigma_1/\\Sigma_2$: dot-dash: 0.5; dash: 0.7; dot: 1; solid: 1.3; thick dot-dash: 1.5; thick dashed: 2; thick dot: 3; thick solid: 4.","source":"arxiv","key":"49f67e2a87f943b10864c696e20ac412","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/49f67e2a87f943b10864c696e20ac412"},{"filename":"rr.png","caption":"Upper panel: ratio of $r$ values at 350 GHz and 150 GHz as a function of cloud 2 temperature, for three different cloud 1 temperatures. Lower panel: Ratio $r=I_1/I_2$ at 150 GHz (black) and 350 GHz (red), as a function of cloud 2 temperature. Cloud 1 temperature is fixed at 20 K. Line type corresponds to different values of $\\Sigma_1/\\Sigma_2$: dot-dash: 0.5; dash: 0.7; dot: 1; solid: 1.3; thick dot-dash: 1.5; thick dashed: 2; thick dot: 3; thick solid: 4.","source":"arxiv","key":"064518de18e96943e1e0f1fc90a3e3c4","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/064518de18e96943e1e0f1fc90a3e3c4"},{"filename":"pp.png","caption":"Ratio of polarization fractions of combined signal as measured at 350 GHz and 150GHz as functions of cloud 2 temperature. Color corresponds to angle $\\alpha$ between the POS magnetic fields of the clouds: black: 90$^\\circ$; blue: $85^\\circ$; red: $80^\\circ$; green: 75$^\\circ$; magenta:$60^\\circ$. Line types as in Fig.~\\ref{roverr} (lower).","source":"arxiv","key":"7d84f75f5812d8241b5eee33c3935935","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/7d84f75f5812d8241b5eee33c3935935"},{"filename":"dchi.png","caption":"Difference of polarization angles of combined signal as measured at 350 GHz and 150 GHz, as a function of cloud 2 temperature. Line types and colors as in Fig.~\\ref{pp}.","source":"arxiv","key":"765fb665581c0b49a6cc47fce1537219","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/765fb665581c0b49a6cc47fce1537219"}],"legacy_version":"20200922210603.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Astrophysics"}],"first_author":{"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/00dr28g20"}],"full_name":"Tassis, Konstantinos","last_name":"Tassis","first_name":"Konstantinos","recid":1037950},"control_number":1325065,"dois":[{"source":"ADS","value":"10.1093/mnrasl/slv077"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Tassis:2014bea"],"abstracts":[{"source":"Oxford University Press","value":"Recent Planck results have shown that radiation from the cosmic microwave background passes through foregrounds in which aligned dust grains produce polarized dust emission, even in regions of the sky with the lowest level of dust emission. One of the most commonly used ways to remove the dust foreground is to extrapolate the polarized dust emission signal from frequencies where it dominates (e.g. ∼350 GHz) to frequencies commonly targeted by cosmic microwave background experiments (e.g. ∼150 GHz). In this Letter, we describe an interstellar medium effect that can lead to decorrelation of the dust emission polarization pattern between different frequencies due to multiple contributions along the line of sight. Using a simple 2-cloud model we show that there are two conditions under which this decorrelation can be large: (a) the ratio of polarized intensities between the two clouds changes between the two frequencies; (b) the magnetic fields between the two clouds contributing along a line of sight are significantly misaligned. In such cases, the 350 GHz polarized sky map is not predictive of that at 150 GHz. We propose a possible correction for this effect, using information from optopolarimetric surveys of dichroicly absorbed starlight.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"Oxford University Press"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"Recent Planck results have shown that radiation from the cosmic microwave background passes through foregrounds in which aligned dust grains produce polarized dust emission, even in regions of the sky with the lowest level of dust emission. One of the most commonly used ways to remove the dust foreground is to extrapolate the polarized dust emission signal from frequencies where it dominates (e.g., ~ 350 GHz) to frequencies commonly targeted by cosmic microwave background experiments (e.g., ~150 GHz). In this paper, we describe an interstellar medium effect that can lead to decorrelation of the dust emission polarization pattern between different frequencies due to multiple contributions along the line of sight. Using a simple 2-cloud model we show that there are two conditions under which this decorrelation can be large: (a) the ratio of polarized intensities between the two clouds changes between the two frequencies; (b) the magnetic fields between the two clouds contributing along a line of sight are significantly misaligned. In such cases, the 350 GHz polarized sky map is not predictive of that at 150 GHz. We propose a possible correction for this effect, using information from optopolarimetric surveys of dichroicly absorbed starlight.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["astro-ph.CO"],"titles":[{"title":"Searching for inflationary B modes: can dust emission properties be extrapolated from 350 GHz to 150 GHz?"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Searching for Inflationary B-modes: Can dust emission properties be extrapolated from 350 GHz to 150 GHz?"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2015-07-21"}],"curated":true}}],"total":2695},"links":{"self":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...dust%20cloud...&size=10&page=1","next":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...dust%20cloud...&size=10&page=2","bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...dust%20cloud...&size=10&page=1&format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...dust%20cloud...&size=10&page=1&format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...dust%20cloud...&size=10&page=1&format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...dust%20cloud...&size=10&page=1&format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...dust%20cloud...&size=10&page=1&format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...dust%20cloud...&size=10&page=1&format=cv"}}